<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Increase Mather</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1639">1639</a>–<a href="/years/1723">1723</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Increase Mather was a Puritan minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>'s North Church and president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard College</a> from 1685 to 1701. He went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1688 to seek the restoration of Massachusetts's charter and in 1691 obtained a new charter from William III and Mary II, which created the Province of Massachusetts Bay. He nominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-phips">William Phips</a> as governor and wrote a cautious critique of the evidence used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salem-witch-trials">Salem witch trials</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/increase-mather.jpg" alt="Increase Mather" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Van Veck Pinx · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Increase_Mather,_1680.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mather was born in Dorchester, in the Massachusetts Bay Colony, on 21 June 1639, the youngest of six sons of the Rev. Richard Mather and Kathrine Holt Mather, who had left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-migration-african-american">Great Migration</a> as nonconformists. His name, a literal translation of the Hebrew Yosëf, was said to recall the increase with which God had favoured the country at his birth. He graduated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard College</a> in 1656, took a Master of Arts at Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, in 1658, and served as chaplain to a garrison in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/channel-islands">Channel Islands</a> until the return of Charles II made him feel insecure there. He sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> in 1661.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> he married his step-sister Maria Cotton, mother of Cotton Mather, born in 1663, and became minister of the North Church, a post he held until his death. A fire on 27 November 1676 destroyed his home and meeting house among 45 buildings. He wrote A Brief History of the War with the Indians in New-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> (1676), an account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-philips-war">King Philip's War</a>, and after watching the comets of 1680 and 1682 published Kometographia (1683). At Harvard he restored the teaching of Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, though he was away from the colony for most of his time as rector.</p><p>When James II revoked the Massachusetts charter in 1686 to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-andros">Edmund Andros</a>, Mather roused opposition, was nearly framed for treason and escaped spies to petition the king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, publishing A Narrative of the Miseries of New-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> (1688). The new charter gave the colony an elected legislature, enfranchised all freeholders and united Massachusetts Bay with Plymouth Colony. He returned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-phips">William Phips</a> on 14 May 1692.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His collection of illustrious providences, published in 1684, had affirmed the real power of witchcraft. On 15 June 1692 he and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> ministers urged caution at Salem and opposed spectral evidence, and his Cases of Conscience Concerning Evil Spirits concluded that it was better for ten suspected witches to escape than for one innocent person to be condemned. At his son's request, however, he added a postscript defending the judges. Because of his part in the trials and his refusal to denounce them, his reputation suffered then and later. Mather owned a slave named Spaniard. He died in Boston on 23 August 1723, aged 84, and was buried in Copp's Hill Burying Ground.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Increase Mather” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the fall of the Dominion of New England, <b>Increase Mather</b> negotiated a new charter from William III and Mary II in 1691.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-massachusetts-bay">Province of Massachusetts Bay</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dorchester, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.32,-71.05&amp;year=1693" aria-label="Dorchester on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2827 658 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Increase Mather"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3277 928h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3277 928h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.32,-71.05&amp;year=1693">What was here in 1693? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It were better that ten suspected Witches should escape, than that one innocent Person should be Condemned; that is an old saying, and true…&#8221;</p><footer>Cases of Conscience Concerning Evil Spirits (1693) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/28513/pg28513-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
